WEBVTT

1
00:00:12.002 --> 00:00:15.083
With a good swat to my mustang Bandit's rump, I'm off.

2
00:00:15.744 --> 00:00:20.845
My name is Henry Thompson; I'm 19 years old, and I'm galloping as fast as I can.

3
00:00:21.505 --> 00:00:27.827
Since April of last year, I have ridden for the Central Overland California and Pike's Peak Express Company,

4
00:00:28.307 --> 00:00:30.268
which is the official name for the Pony Express.

5
00:00:31.132 --> 00:00:36.819
My route in Nevada, from Fort Churchill to Friday's Station, is only one part of a large network

6
00:00:36.859 --> 00:00:42.325
of routes that have made it possible to send news, letters, and other important papers all the way

7
00:00:42.385 --> 00:00:43.206
across the country.

8
00:00:43.807 --> 00:00:47.391
I'm proud to be a part of the Pony Express, which takes the mail from St.

9
00:00:47.431 --> 00:00:51.035
Joseph, Missouri, to San Francisco, California, in about ten days.

10
00:00:52.364 --> 00:01:00.293
But today, November 17, 1861, is my last ride, because the telegraph line joining both coasts of America

11
00:01:00.633 --> 00:01:02.736
went into operation about three weeks ago.

12
00:01:03.417 --> 00:01:06.260
Soon there will be no more need for the Pony Express.

13
00:01:06.961 --> 00:01:12.687
Eighteen months ago I was picked from the many young men who answered a newspaper advertisement in Sacramento,

14
00:01:12.707 --> 00:01:21.246
California. The company wanted skilled riders weighing less than 120 pounds, which describes me perfectly, since I'm skinny

15
00:01:21.286 --> 00:01:23.086
as a rail and an excellent rider.

16
00:01:23.687 --> 00:01:28.228
The weight the horses carry must be as low as possible because they run very hard.

17
00:01:28.888 --> 00:01:33.929
The horses we ride aren't really ponies, but they're small, which is probably why the company got the

18
00:01:33.969 --> 00:01:35.250
nickname "Pony Express."

19
00:01:36.210 --> 00:01:41.912
To make it easier on our horses, I'm only allowed to carry 25 pounds of equipment, like some

20
00:01:42.032 --> 00:01:43.392
food and my Colt revolver.

21
00:01:44.168 --> 00:01:49.352
Of course I also carry an additional twenty pounds of mail in a mochila, a leather saddlebag.

22
00:01:49.972 --> 00:01:54.055
I was assigned to the Fort Churchill home station, and I've learned my route well.

23
00:01:54.735 --> 00:01:59.178
It's one of the hardest routes, because I have to go high into the Sierra Nevada Mountains.

24
00:02:00.059 --> 00:02:04.682
You'd think the Pony Express carries the mail only by horse, but that's not exactly true.

25
00:02:05.202 --> 00:02:09.365
The mail usually travels between San Francisco and Sacramento by boat.

26
00:02:09.805 --> 00:02:11.066
Between the east and St.

27
00:02:11.126 --> 00:02:13.888
Joseph, Missouri, the mail travels by stagecoach.

28
00:02:14.572 --> 00:02:16.815
But riders on horseback are needed between St.

29
00:02:16.835 --> 00:02:23.162
Joseph and Sacramento because it's rough country— through the deserts of the Great Basin and over the mountains.

30
00:02:24.263 --> 00:02:31.071
There are home stations, where the riders live between trips, every 75 to 100 miles, and relay stations,

31
00:02:31.332 --> 00:02:34.095
where the riders can change horses, every 10 to 15 miles.

32
00:02:35.312 --> 00:02:40.014
Saddling up a fresh horse usually takes less than two minutes, and then we're off again to the

33
00:02:40.074 --> 00:02:41.055
next relay station.

34
00:02:41.635 --> 00:02:45.096
My route is about 75 miles, but other routes are longer.

35
00:02:45.997 --> 00:02:49.098
Special, lightweight saddles make the horses' jobs easier.

36
00:02:49.698 --> 00:02:53.340
I sling the mochila over the saddle and secure it over the saddle's horn.

37
00:02:54.040 --> 00:02:59.323
Most of the time we carry about fifty pieces of mail, which are placed in four leather boxes

38
00:02:59.823 --> 00:03:01.864
sewn into the corners of the mochila.

39
00:03:02.580 --> 00:03:07.564
When the Pony Express started, the price to deliver a piece of mail was five dollars for each

40
00:03:07.704 --> 00:03:08.305
half ounce.

41
00:03:09.346 --> 00:03:14.871
Only the wealthiest people use our service; although we've dropped the price to one dollar now, that's still

42
00:03:14.891 --> 00:03:15.511
a lot of money.

43
00:03:16.272 --> 00:03:18.614
Our service is expensive because it's the quickest.

44
00:03:19.414 --> 00:03:26.752
Before the Pony Express, mail from New York to San Francisco took about four weeks—and often longer— because

45
00:03:26.892 --> 00:03:32.755
it had to be shipped down the East Coast to the Isthmus of Panama, transported by rail overland,

46
00:03:33.255 --> 00:03:35.716
and then shipped up the West Coast by steamship.

47
00:03:36.656 --> 00:03:43.819
But President Abraham Lincoln's inaugural address made it to California in 7 days and 17 hours— because it

48
00:03:43.859 --> 00:03:47.540
was telegraphed to Nebraska and then traveled on the Pony Express.

49
00:03:48.641 --> 00:03:53.943
The rider from the east arrived at Fort Churchill at six o'clock tonight, and I left right away.

50
00:03:54.463 --> 00:03:56.084
I'm so grateful the moon is full.

51
00:03:57.332 --> 00:04:02.014
Although I know this route like the back of my hand, I don't want anything bad to happen

52
00:04:02.074 --> 00:04:03.114
on my last ride.

53
00:04:03.755 --> 00:04:09.037
As I head toward Reed's Station, I remember the day I rode through a forest of aspens, where

54
00:04:09.177 --> 00:04:10.077
Indians were hiding.

55
00:04:10.677 --> 00:04:15.599
I crouched over my horse's neck and rode as fast as I could, shooting behind me the whole

56
00:04:15.659 --> 00:04:19.361
time— that was the fastest I have ever ridden in my whole life.

57
00:04:20.141 --> 00:04:25.644
As I get close to Reed's Station, I blow a horn four times to alert the stationmaster that

58
00:04:25.664 --> 00:04:26.224
I'm coming in.

59
00:04:26.996 --> 00:04:30.658
A fresh horse is ready for me, and I immediately set off for Dayton.

60
00:04:31.499 --> 00:04:35.461
That little town sprang up just recently because gold was discovered nearby.

61
00:04:36.201 --> 00:04:41.204
Riding through a hemlock forest, I have to be careful my horse doesn't stumble in the dark, because

62
00:04:41.244 --> 00:04:42.845
a few riders have been hurt this way.

63
00:04:43.726 --> 00:04:48.188
But once we're out of the woods, there are places where I can let him run as fast

64
00:04:48.248 --> 00:04:50.470
as he wants on this chilly, moonlit night.

65
00:04:51.050 --> 00:04:56.624
Pony Express horses are specially selected for their speed and endurance, and they can gallop up to 25

66
00:04:56.925 --> 00:05:02.386
miles per hour; usually, though, we ride at about 10 miles per hour to save the horses' energy.

67
00:05:03.406 --> 00:05:09.508
After a brief stop at Carson City, it's a fairly easy 15-mile ride to Genoa, but the hardest

68
00:05:09.548 --> 00:05:11.109
part of my trip is still ahead of me.

69
00:05:11.889 --> 00:05:15.470
With a fresh horse, I head into the Sierra Nevada Mountains.

70
00:05:16.430 --> 00:05:22.012
This stretch of the ride is only about ten miles, but the uphill climb is extremely difficult.

71
00:05:22.652 --> 00:05:28.249
Halfway up I find myself in six inches of snow, and I must slow down— slipping on the

72
00:05:28.309 --> 00:05:30.570
rocky slopes would be a costly delay.

73
00:05:31.470 --> 00:05:36.332
Soon, though, I see the light reflecting off Lake Tahoe, and both my horse and I are relieved

74
00:05:36.372 --> 00:05:42.894
to finally arrive at our destination, Friday's Station, and pass the mochila on to the next rider.

75
00:05:43.574 --> 00:05:48.055
Now I'm going to take off my boots, grab a bowl of beans, and sit by the fire

76
00:05:48.095 --> 00:05:51.176
as I think of all the men that made the Pony Express possible.

77
00:05:52.088 --> 00:05:56.712
Some are no longer with us, killed by Indians or robbers or the weather.

78
00:05:57.493 --> 00:06:01.737
After I rest a bit, I'll probably head east, where I hear the Union Army is looking for

79
00:06:01.777 --> 00:06:02.598
some good riders.

80
00:06:03.418 --> 00:06:06.421
After being a Pony Express rider, I'm used to danger.

81
00:06:06.922 --> 00:06:09.304
It's time for me to look for a new adventure.
